From 2f9eb52bf806af22594aaeed895a78f150a91c43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:54:21 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 48/49] super-amd: change the chunk of a RAID5 or a RAID10

mdadm --grow <RAID5> --chunk=K (with --raid-devices=N or not) and
<RAID10> --chunk=K, K one of the menu's 64, 128 and 256 KiB that divides
the members.  AMD's own software never changes the chunk (rcblob
RC_CreateTransformRaidArray gives the target the source's CacheTagSize),
but its transform engine carries such a migration out: the same LD with
the new CacheTagSize, the data of each element moving from +0x5000 down
to +0x800, the flags of a migration onto more disks
(RC_SetupTransformParams: as many data disks, the data at +0x5000) -
rcraid 9.3.3 takes one forged so to the end.  It is recorded and run as
adding disks to a RAID5 is: md reshapes moving data_offset, held behind
the transform log region by region, mdmon writes "100 %" and the final
LD as md ends.

The region is RC_TransformProtectSize of the transform, the stripes of
both LDs counted in the chunk of the source: for a RAID5 the same as of
the target, for a RAID10 of three columns it is not (0x7f80 from 64
KiB).  md counts a RAID10 reshape in sectors of the array and records
its position in sync_completed only at its own checkpoints, not while
sync_max holds it: manage_reshape_amd() reads how far it got from
/proc/mdstat (the position less the sync I/O in flight), only while md
reshapes (sync_action) and never the whole array: md interrupted
(mdadm -S) shows there the whole array done for some 15 ms as it
cleans up (md.c status_resync()) - sync_action reads reshape then,
unless md was frozen first (Grow.c abort_reshape() writes "idle") -,
which taken would log the next region as reached, one md never moved
(hardware: md held at 0xb0000 of 0x400000 showed 0x400000 so).  md at
the end shows no reshape position, which ends the wait.  At assembly md
gets the old chunk and the new one; md's end of it is the target's
disks and chunk.

Tested on an AMD X870E board: a RAID5 of 3 disks 64 -> 128 KiB and a
RAID10 2 x 2 64 -> 256 KiB - md check clean, the data the same, the BIOS
menu shows them Normal with the new Cache Tag Size, the AMD Windows
driver reads the same data; the RAID5 one stopped midway the Windows
driver finished itself, and one stopped before its first region md
went on with from 0 at the next assembly.  With rcraid 9.3.3 in a QEMU
guest: either finishes the other's (stopped midway, RAID5 and RAID10),
rcraid takes ours finished as NORMAL with the new chunk, and two forged
as the AMD driver would record them, midway in one container, md
finishes one after the other, the final LDs those of rcraid.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model by Anthropic.
